1bf52945b9fa01496ce41a349f8eaace490ad4ac:docs/plans/funding-opportunities-makegov-v1/completion-matrix.md:246:| 2026-08-07 | **The already-merged Phase 7 Workstream D migration (`20260822010000_pe_budget_phase7_canonical_promotion`, PE budget program — not this program's own work) had never successfully applied to `capiro-dev`, and was silently blocking P44/P45's migrations behind it.** | Its `ALTER ROLE capiro_pe_promoter NOSUPERUSER NOCREATEDB NOCREATEROLE NOREPLICATION NOBYPASSRLS` line requires the *calling* role to itself hold real `SUPERUSER`. Aurora's master user only holds the restricted `rds_superuser` pseudo-role, so this line failed every real Aurora deploy with `permission denied to alter role`, and since Prisma runs each migration file in one transaction, the whole migration (13 tables, the role, every grant) rolled back — every time, silently, for as long as this migration has existed. It never surfaced in local rehearsal because a local Postgres superuser really does hold `SUPERUSER`. | Fixed in [PR #815](https://github.com/neo-capiro/capirov2/pull/815): the `ALTER ROLE` line is dropped entirely (every attribute it named is already `CREATE ROLE`'s own default), verified by a clean 245-migration replay confirming the role still ends up `rolsuper=f, rolcreatedb=f, rolcreaterole=f, rolreplication=f, rolbypassrls=f`. Recovery required a documented, two-person-overridden `prisma migrate resolve --rolled-back` per `docs/runbooks/prisma-failed-migration-recovery.md` (a new `migrate-resolve-rolled-back`/`migrate-resolve-applied` entrypoint verb pair was added in [PR #816](https://github.com/neo-capiro/capirov2/pull/816) so that recovery never touches `_prisma_migrations` by hand) — executed against `capiro-dev` 2026-08-07, after which `migrate deploy` applied this migration plus its queue-link migration plus **both P44 and P45's migrations** in one run. Collateral damage from the fix's own wording (the word "real" in a prose comment false-matched a structural test's `\bREAL\b` no-floating-point regex) plus pre-existing CRLF-vs-LF drift on this migration file (`.sql` has no `eol=lf` rule in `.gitattributes`, unlike `.sh`/`.ts`/`.md`) broke 4 more assertions in `canonical-promotion-schema-v1.spec.ts` on a Windows checkout; all fixed in the same PR by normalizing the spec's own read of the file to LF rather than touching the applied migration's committed bytes. |
